2. WHO ICD-10 and ICD-O-3

The base codes under vocab/condition/ICD_10 are referenced from
WHO's International Statistical Classification of Diseases, 10th
revision; the tree carries codes and hierarchy only — no WHO designation
text is present in the committed corpus. The ICD-10 crosswalk collections
(icd-10-snomed-ct, icd-10-meddra, icd-10-orphanet, icd-10-omop-snomed-ct,
icd-10-omop-ext) are likewise not committed: WHO's ICD-10 license reserves
crosswalks to ICD-10 to a separate written agreement, so that mapping
surface lives in the operator's local imports/ directory and merges
into the tool's local readings only through its --imports option.
ICD-10, ICF, and ICD-O-3 remain subject to WHO's own terms. The WHO ICD-10
Volume 2 (6th edition, 2019) is published under CC BY-ND 3.0 IGO, whose
license does not cover mappings or crosslinks to ICD-10 ("subject to a
separate written agreement from WHO"); this tree therefore claims no
grant over WHO content and relicenses nothing of it. The ICD-O-3
morphology codes under vocab/condition/ICD_O_3 are carried as a
code-only placeholder inventory in the same position; the classification
belongs to WHO and remains subject to its terms.

3. Orphanet

Orphanet is referenced under CC BY 4.0
(https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/): free to reference and
share with attribution, as recorded beside its system declaration in
_registry/external.kdl.

The ICD-10-SE to Orphanet mappings (icd-10-se-orphanet) are derived from
Orphanet's ICD-10 alignment, Orphadata en_product1, data version
2026-06-23, accessed 2026-11-04: each Swedish extension code inherits
its ICD-10 parent's exact alignment as a narrower claim. They carry
ORPHAcodes only, no Orphanet text. Attribution: "Orphanet: an online
rare disease and orphan drug data base. © INSERM 1999. Available on
http://www.orpha.net."

4. Reference-only terminologies: codes, never text

SNOMED CT and MedDRA are licensed terminologies whose license records
in _registry/external.kdl mark them `distribution=reference-only` with
redistribution prohibited. LOINC is likewise declared
`distribution=reference-only` but carries no license record in the
tree: LOINC terms remain governed by the Regenstrief LOINC license.
None of the three has its text copied into this tree: their codes
appear only as mapping endpoints, and each remains governed by its own
license (SNOMED CT Affiliate License, the Regenstrief LOINC license,
the MedDRA license held by the MSSO). Use of those codes requires your
own license with the respective owner.

This material includes SNOMED Clinical Terms® (SNOMED CT®) which is
used by permission of the International Health Terminology Standards
Development Organisation (IHTSDO). All rights reserved. The SNOMED CT
Swedish extension (edition SE1000052) shares the SNOMED position: it is
declared `SNOMED_SE_EXT` as reference-only, its concepts are referenced
by SCTID alone, and its licensed synonyms and descriptions are never
copied here.

5. UCUM

UCUM codes are referenced verbatim as mapping endpoints; the UCUM
standard and its unit tables are not copied into this tree. The
UCUM Copyright Notice and License 1.1 (https://ucum.org/license) is
recorded as `org.regenstrief.ucum` with redistribution conditional.
Codes used as mapping endpoints interoperate with the UCUM Work and are
not derivatives of it.

6. Other external systems; OMOP; RxNorm

ATC, NPL, EDQM, and ICD-10-CM are public systems referenced by
code; no license constraint is recorded against them in the tree. OMOP_EXT
(official OHDSI Athena extension concept ids) is likewise declared
`distribution=reference-only` — referenced by numeric concept id only,
with no English labels and no standard flags — with no license record;
OMOP_CUSTOM is this project's own id space and claims nothing
from OHDSI. RxNorm (`drug/RXNORM/`) is produced by
the U.S. National Library of Medicine; the RxNorm normalized names and
codes are in the public domain as works of the U.S. Government. The
rxnorm-atc collection carries RxNorm codes and product names with
acknowledgement of NLM; this tree is a snapshot (2026.1) and does not
reflect the most current RxNorm data available from NLM, and nothing
here implies NLM endorsement.

7. NCSP

The codes under vocab/intervention/NCSP are carried from the
NOMESCO Classification of Surgical Procedures (NCSP), version 1.16
(2011), maintained by the Nordic Medico-Statistical Committee (NOMESCO)
under the Nordic Council of Ministers, which claims copyright in the
classification. This tree carries the code identifiers only — the
official English designations are not present in the committed corpus —
for interoperability with the Nordic procedure classifications the
Swedish KVÅ surgical codes extend. No public reuse license over the
classification has been found; nothing here claims a grant over it, and
its includes/excludes notes and internal relationships are not copied
here.

8. TLV (statutory basis); the Swedish national classifications

The TLV registers (drug/TLV_VNR/, drug/TLV_FBA/, drug/TLV_VGR/,
drug/TLV_VNR_DOSE_FORM/, drug/ATC_YCODE/) are carried as code
identifiers: TLV publishes these datasamlingar as open data
"för vidareutnyttjande" under lag (2022:818) om företag som förmedlar
läkemedel m.m. (https://tlv.se/om-tlv/oppna-data); no explicit license
accompanies them. This tree carries no TLV product, article, or group
designation catalogue — codes, hierarchy, and this project's own
mapping assertions — and claims no grant over TLV's records; the
list's undantag column is carried only as the flags' `scope` where the
listing narrows.

The Swedish national classifications — ICD-10-SE, KVÅ (KKÅ and KMÅ),
the Swedish ICF, and KSI, published by E-hälsomyndigheten since
1 June 2026, and SPKOD, published by Socialstyrelsen — are carried as
codes, hierarchy, and validity; their designation catalogues are not
redistributed. ICF is WHO's classification; Socialstyrelsen holds the
rights to its Swedish translation.

Quotation as review evidence. Where a mapping was judged in review, its
rationale may quote the single title it judged from the Swedish
national classifications or TLV's published lists, with the source
named, under the quotation right of the Swedish Copyright Act
(lag (1960:729) om upphovsrätt till litterära och konstnärliga verk,
22 §, with attribution under 11 §). These quotations remain the
publishers' material; they are not licensed here under CC BY 4.0.
RETTS triage titles are not quoted (see 10).

9. NPU; SCB; the local laboratory material

The `lab/NPU/` codes are reproduced from the public NPU terminology
service of Equalis AB (https://npu.equalis.se): for each code, the
IFCC/IUPAC property definition, the report name, and the measurement
unit — nothing else. The NPU Terminology is the property of the
IFCC/IUPAC committee on Nomenclature for Properties and Units and is
published at www.labterm.dk; the Swedish SWE codes are the national
extensions maintained by Equalis. The designation strings are
reproduced as published for interoperability, associated with their
NPU codes, and the tree claims no grant over the registry itself.

Geographic area codes (geo/GEO_SE/), civil-status codes
(demo/CIVIL_STATUS_SE/), and education codes (demo/LISA_EDUCATION/) come from
Statistics Sweden (SCB) registers that SCB publishes as open data under
Creative Commons CC0 1.0 Universal; the Swedish names of those codes
are carried as published, with credit to SCB. No English municipal or
county names are carried; SCB publishes no English names.

The local laboratory catalogue titles in lab/LAB_SE/ and the unit
spellings in lab/UNIT_SE/ originate in the GMLF research project's
evaluation of the laboratory data it had access to, and its attempt to
map that data to OMOP. They are not drawn from a public register. They
are published here by Ludvig Aldén; their canonicalization, mappings,
and assessments in this tree are the author's own work.

10. RETTS triage

The triage/TRIAGE_SE/ codes are carried from the RETTS triage
instrument (© Omda Predicare AB) as code identifiers only — the RETTS
chief-complaint and priority designations are not present in the
committed corpus — for interoperability. Nothing here claims a grant
over RETTS; the instrument remains subject to its owner's terms.
